    "note": "HUMAN_VERIFIED — approved by the repository owner (@marinoscar) on 2026-09-04 (issue #261). Basis of approval, recorded precisely so a later reader can judge its weight: USCIS publishes reading and writing VOCABULARY LISTS and no sentence list at all (docs/specs/english-test.md §1.1, quoting M-1178 (09/17): \"create as many interrogative sentences (or questions) as you can, using only the words on the list\"), so the 36 sentences below — 16 reading, 20 writing — were COMPOSED against the two vocabulary files checked in beside this one, never transcribed from a source that contains them; each sentence's own provenance triple therefore points at the vocabulary list it was composed from, not at a document it appears in. What is mechanically enforced, on every test run and again before a single row is written: validateEnglishContent (prisma/content/english-vocabulary.ts) tokenises every sentence through the full normalizeAnswer pipeline (with the leading-article sentinel, so \"the\" and \"a\" are checked rather than silently dropped) and raises an `error` for any token that does not appear on the vocabulary list matching that sentence's own kind — reading against the reading list, writing against the writing list, never their union — and for a duplicate (kind, ordinal); load-english-content.ts runs that validator FIRST and throws before opening its transaction if any error is present, so off-list content cannot reach the database, and the very same word-by-word pass derives each sentence's vocab_tags, which is why a tag cannot drift from the words it describes. english-content-validator.spec.ts runs that check over these real files (currently zero errors across all 36 sentences) and english-content-loader.spec.ts covers the loader. What was NOT done, stated plainly rather than implied: no comparison against the official USCIS vocabulary PDFs was performed for this approval and none was possible — uscis.gov returns HTTP 403 and web.archive.org is unreachable from the environment this work was done in — so the sha256/retrievedAt values recorded here and in the two vocabulary files were carried forward verbatim from the earlier session that wrote them and were NOT re-derived from a freshly downloaded document; and there was no page-by-page human re-read of anything. These sentences were also NOT hand-composed: they were produced by an unattended agent session (issue #130, PR #250), whose note in this field claimed they were \"composed by hand … then reviewed word by word\" — a human process this repository has no evidence of. That claim is withdrawn and replaced by this one; docs/specs/english-test.md §1.2 still requires human composition for sentences added from here on, and the status HUMAN_COMPOSED_AND_REVIEWED remains the honest label for a file that genuinely earns it. What this status does NOT certify: (1) that any sentence is natural, idiomatic, correctly punctuated, or of a register a real officer would use — that judgement rests on the owner's own reading on the date above, not on any recorded review; (2) that reading sentences are interrogative and writing sentences declarative — §1.2 requires it and every sentence here does satisfy it, but NOTHING in the validator checks it, so this too rests on the owner's reading; (3) that no two sentences collapse to the same normalised text (§1.2's \"President of the United States\" quirk) — the validator checks duplicate ordinals only, never duplicate content; (4) that the two vocabulary lists these were composed against are themselves faithful to the official PDFs — each vocabulary file's own transcription.warning carries that same caveat, and this file inherits it wholesale."
  },
